/usr/local/lib/sp1/tests - the test and diagnostic programs of service
pack 1, built static for Atari System V.  Most need root; those marked
"screen" or "registers" change the hardware's state and say how they
put it back.  Sources and notes: github.com/slaapliedje/atari-sysv-sp1
(tools/snd, tools/atw, tools/tram, atw800, driver-midi, tools/setboot).

The TT
  khz              the kernel's HZ and the rate of its clock tick (lbolt),
                   measured over 5 s
  cachet           cycles per loop from registers, a cache-sized array and
                   a big one: whether the 68030's caches are on and how
                   fast this process's memory is
  mixbench         what software audio mixing costs on this CPU

DMA sound (/dev/audio, the snd driver)
  sndprobe         the sound hardware's registers
  sndstat          the driver's state and statistics
  sndrate          2 s of silence at each rate, timed by the clock
                   (6258, 12517, 25033, 50066 Hz)
  sndtest [RATE]   2 s of 440 Hz mono, 2 s of 440/660 stereo, a 2 s
                   sweep: listen

MIDI (/dev/midi, /dev/midit, the midi driver)
  miditool raw HEX...   bytes to /dev/midi
  miditool phrase       C E G C on /dev/midi, the program waits
  miditool timed        the same in one write to /dev/midit, timed by
                        the kernel
  miditool flush        4 s of notes queued, flushed after 1 s: MIDI_FLUSH

ATW800/2 video (reads only unless said)
  atwprobe         which 1 MB windows of 0xFEA00000-0xFEDFFFFF answer and
                   where the card's id block is
  atw4mb [keep]    registers: whether register 15 = 3 gives a real 4 MB;
                   puts the 2 MB layout back unless told to keep it
  atwstart LO HI SECONDS   registers: moves the display start (VTG 12/13)
                   for SECONDS, then back to 0
  atwbars 16|32    screen: 640x480 colour bars at 16 or 32 bits a pixel;
                   restore the desktop with atwtest or a restart of X
  atwtest, atwtest.sh   screen: the 16 and 32 bpp byte-order test screens,
                   then X's 1024x768 8 bpp mode and the 2 MB layout
                   back (the .sh adds xrefresh: run it from an xterm)
  atwfb            screen: 640x480x256 with a test pattern, the card's
                   first light from user space (via /dev/atw or /dev/mem)
  atwblit          the 2D engine's copy and fill commands, byte-checked
  atwblstat        the engine's status word at every register offset
  atwblspeed       the engine's copy and fill throughput against the CPU
  atwfill          what a fill takes as its source
  atwtorture       300 random unaligned copies and fills against a CPU
                   model (off-screen memory)

Transputers (/dev/link0 = the C011 link to TRAM slot 1, /dev/link1 =
the FPGA's T425; the tlk driver).  Each resets the transputer it talks to;
a Helios or other session on it is lost.
  tprobe           (in /usr/local/bin, run at boot) what answers on each
                   link: POKE/PEEK and the type probe
  ttest [-p PACE] [/dev/linkN]   POKE/PEEK, the type probe, then the
                   manual's read test: throughput through the driver
  tdiag [/dev/linkN]   where a link's time goes, byte by byte, timed
                   inside the driver (TLK_DIAG)
  tmem [-r] [/dev/linkN]   how much memory the transputer has, by
                   POKE/PEEK at 2^n; -r: the one beyond relay.tas
  tfpu [-n] [-r] [/dev/linkN]   whether the transputer has an FPU, and
                   its lddevid; -n skips the FPU instructions, -r reaches
                   the second transputer through the first
  tspy [/dev/linkN]   which of the transputer's links 1-3 lead somewhere

NVRAM
  setboot          (in /usr/local/bin) the TT's boot preference:
                   setboot tos | unix | none, no argument prints it
